## Context

Goal 2 of the ladder (`README.md`): *the cost of staying current is what
changed, two server processes on one data directory do not corrupt or duplicate,
and an index under another schema version ends up served.* Three terms: **R3,
R13, R23**. Second rung because these need a built index but not a good one.

This tracker holds one question: **is goal 2 kept for zoteus?** Today no clause
of it has been decided at all.

## Where the rung stands for zoteus

From `bench/results/0604-ladder-matrix/MATRIX.md`, 2026-09-03, revision 1.12.0 —
every cell `not-run`, and the reasons differ:

| clause | why not decided |
|---|---|
| `R3-edit-recomputes-only-what-changed` | no `work.<stage>.<trigger>.<outcome>` counters to read |
| `R3-identical-resync-recomputes-nothing` | idem |
| `R13-two-processes-both-answer` | no process reported a hit list |
| `R13-two-processes-do-not-duplicate-work` | no counters |
| `R23-foreign-stamp-ends-up-serving` | the target answers without reporting what it matched |

Four of five turn on one absence, which is why the counters are the largest
single unblock on the whole board and not only this rung's.

## The gap this tracker owns that 0033 does not

**A non-destructive resume, required by R3 and R4 — not by R22.** zoteus's only
action that continues an interrupted build is also the full rebuild, and on a
damaged index it deletes the unreadable file first. Continuing therefore
re-embeds every byte that did not change, which is the sentence R3 exists to
forbid: *"The cost of staying current MUST be proportional to what changed,
never to the size of the library … MUST re-embed nothing whose bytes are
unchanged."* Deleting the index before rebuilding also strains R4's *"the index
MUST answer queries at every moment of its life"*.

Recorded because it was nearly mis-filed: the ratified seven-verb interface
names `resume`, and the adapter declares it absent, which invites reading the
gap as an interface gap on goal 1's rung. **An interface verb is not a promise.**
The promise is R3's, and R3 sits here.

**And it is the hard one of the three, for a reason worth stating.** The other
two gaps found by the 2026-09-03 reread are contained: an uninstall surface
removes a declared set of paths, and R22's restart clause needs a measurement
before it needs any code at all. A resume that neither rebuilds nor deletes is
not contained — it requires the system to know, per item and per stage, what was
finished, what was in flight when the stop arrived, and what is downstream of a
key that has since changed. That is the same substrate the counters need: a
ledger whose unit of invalidation is (item × stage), which is R3's own body
("the unit of invalidation is (item × stage)") and is what ticket 0033 builds.

So gap 3 and the counters are **one piece of work seen from two sides** — resume
asks the ledger *what is left to do*, the counters ask it *what has been done* —
and neither is a small contained contribution of the kind `SYNC.md` records
going upstream as a pull request. That argues for the issue form the maintainer
builds himself, and it argues against scheduling gap 3 as though it were a
sibling of the uninstall surface.

## Depends on

- **ticket 0033** — the ledger, freshness and counters. Four of this rung's five
  cells cannot be read until it lands. This tracker does not restate its scope.
- **ticket 0613** — goal 1. Not a blocker in the erg sense: the rungs are ordered
  by build order, not by dependency, and README records that the durability rung
  precedes goal 1 in one direction while goal 1 is numbered first.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] all five goal-2 clauses green for zoteus, each resting on a run
- [ ] the resume gap has a decided upstream form and is filed, or is recorded as
      deliberately not filed with the reason

## Why one tracker per rung, per target

The ladder's rungs are conjunctions: a rung is kept when every one of its members
holds, and at no state before that. Nothing in the repository asserted that
conjunction *for a named target* — `README.md` reports each requirement's
standing across the project, and `bench/results/0604-ladder-matrix/MATRIX.md`
reports each clause against each target, but neither answers "is this rung kept
for zoteus". These three trackers answer exactly that, one each, and they close
only when their rung's every clause is green for zoteus.

zoteus is the named target because *works for me* is the acceptance standard and
this is the tool the author runs. A green here is a green for zoteus and for
nothing else; the other four roster targets are measured by the same layer and
are not this tracker's business.

## Children

Children are filed when their work starts, not up front. The gaps below are
enumerated with the requirement sentence each rests on, which is what a child
ticket would carry anyway; filing four stubs today would inflate the queue
without advancing anything (severity floor, `rules/workflow.md`). This tracker is
edited to list each child as it is created.

